Metropolis-Hastings MCMC — standalone browser demonstration
=========================================================

Group: Bayesian Statistics & Posterior Inference

Open index.html directly, or serve this repository with any static web server.
Keep MCMC/ beside TeachingTools/: the shared CSS, Canvas helpers and numerical
utilities are local relative assets. No Shiny, WebR, packages, fonts, CDNs or
runtime downloads are needed. app.R remains the unchanged historical reference.

Models and priors
-----------------
Infer two parameters from reproducible simulated observations:
- Gaussian means, with known common data SD and correlation.
- Binomial probability p and integer draw count N.
- Beta shapes alpha and beta.
- Gamma shape and rate (not scale).

Uniform priors cover the specified independent ranges. The reference app's
unimplemented Gaussian-prior option is now explicitly a bounded Gaussian:
mean = range midpoint, SD = width / 4, truncated to the range. On N this is a
normalised discrete distribution over the allowed integers. Prior endpoints
must increase; positive shapes/rates exclude zero; singular Gaussian
correlations and incompatible Binomial ranges produce visible errors.

Sampling and playback
---------------------
The default preserves the reference's Gaussian random walk, with a symmetric
{-1, 0, +1} integer increment for Binomial N. Proposal SD consistently means SD
(the R continuous proposal passed its labelled SD as a covariance).

Optional independent global proposals and local/global mixtures demonstrate
the Hastings correction. Continuous global proposals have midpoint means and
SD = prior width / 3; global N is uniform on the allowed integers. Mixtures use
the total forward/reverse mixture densities, not just the chosen component.
Proposals outside target support are rejected without clipping or redrawing.
Every attempt records a state, including repeated states after rejection.
There is no adaptation; the page explains why uncontrolled adaptation is not
equivalent to stationary Metropolis-Hastings.

One to eight seeded chains start at dispersed, valid points. Data and sampler
use separate seeded streams; sampler-only changes leave observations fixed.
The same settings reproduce all data, starts, proposals and acceptance draws.
JavaScript and R generators are different, so paths are not identical to R.

Playback starts paused. Next iteration, Play/Pause, Rewind, Show all iterations
and Seek reveal the cached chain, without resampling. The focused chain shows
the attempted proposal, uniform acceptance draw, and the accepted move or
rejection/return. Model and sampler changes regenerate and rewind. Burn-in,
thinning, focused chain, timing, trails, points/lines and prior-boundary display
do not regenerate.

Diagnostics
-----------
Discarding B means removing the first B attempted transitions, never counting
the initial point as a retained sample. Thinning keeps B+1, B+1+k, ... . Only
iterations revealed by playback enter diagnostics.

Trace plots, retained histograms (probability bars for N), sample moments,
within-chain ACFs and lag plots preserve repeated states. Full-target reference
marginals and moments use nonuniform midpoint quadrature over the complete
prior box, and an exact finite sum over allowed N values. The quadrature is an
approximation, not a Gaussian fitted to the samples; finite-grid accuracy can
deteriorate for extremely concentrated/boundary targets. Target density shading
is relative density, not a credible region. Dense joint clouds draw at most
1,500 states per chain for responsiveness; summaries use every retained state.

ACFs are averaged within chains, never across concatenated chain boundaries.
Split R-hat requires at least two chains with four retained states each and
is undefined without within-chain variation. Approximate ACF ESS uses positive
lag pairs through at most lag 40 and can be optimistic with slow mixing. No
diagnostic or high acceptance rate proves convergence; thinning does not create
information and burn-in does not remove dependence.

Files and validation
--------------------
- index.html: accessible native controls, responsive page and teaching text.
- model.js: pure numerical kernel; browser MCMCModel and CommonJS exports.
- app.js: cached playback, validation and Canvas 2D views.
- test_mcmc.js: node:test algorithm, support, quadrature and stationary checks.
- test_browser.js: shared Chrome harness; playback, settings, plots, errors,
  keyboard tabs, responsive/DPR rendering, file:// and local-only assets.

Run with Node 22 or newer:
  node --test MCMC/test_mcmc.js
  node MCMC/test_browser.js

The browser test needs Chrome (CHROME_BIN may override its path). Its owned
Chrome profile stays inside MCMC/.browser-test/ and is cleaned up afterwards.

